this year we saw no shortage of  experimental indie projects and  blockbusters that defied convention to  shock audiences want to watch a film  that has you wide-eyed in open mouth by  the time the credits start to roll check  out a few of the most baffling movies of  2018 Suspiria may be a remake but this  modern take on Dario Argento's 1977 film  stands apart from other 2018 horror  films  when Suzy played by Dakota Johnson is  admitted to a dance academy in West  Berlin the Academy is still reeling over  the disappearance of a student before  her disappearance the student confessed  that she believed the Academy was run by  a coven of witches and as it turns out  she might not have been crazy after all  [Applause]  Suspiria is a disturbing body horror  packed with trippy imagery it's like  watching a feature shot in a Hall of  Mirrors  told in six distinct narrative acts the  film makes it difficult to distinguish  what's real and what's merely a product  of the characters imaginations starring  lucky Stanfield is Cassius green and  Tessa Thompson as his artistic  girlfriend Detroit sorry to bother you  begins as a film about a desperate  telemarketer trying to pay his rent but  turns into an absurdist dark comedy that  subverts all expectations by the third  act as his co-workers unionised to  protest their low pay Cassius tells  himself that he can support them from  the other side of the picket line but he  gets swept up into a conspiracy that he  never saw coming  sorry to bother you is easily one of the  strangest and best movies in decades  starring Nicolas Cage as red and Andrea  Riseborough is his titular companion  Mandy is easily some of Kage's best work  in recent years the plot follows red and  Mandy as they live a happy reclusive  life until Mandy is kidnapped by a  violent hippie cult directed by Panos  cosmatos Mandy delves into gory  psychedelic imagery that you can't look  away from as the film progresses the  line between fantasy and reality is so  heavily blurred that you're left on sure  of what to believe  assassination nation quickly builds into  something much more sinister and  terrifying than you could have ever  expected when a hacker starts revealing  the personal messages of a small town  the airing of dirty laundry turns from  petty highschool drama into a full-blown  conflict assassination nation takes a  predictable premise and jacks it all the  way up to the most extreme possible  conclusion as more secrets are revealed  the degradation of social norms occurs  so quickly that it's safe to assume it  wouldn't be a realistic turn of events  in real life but it's worth asking what  if an first reformed Reverend Ernst  Stoller holds services at a church with  a dwindling congregation as he's  grappling with the death of his son  Toller gets a frantic call from one of  his flock she's worried about her  husband Michael a radical  environmentalist who sees no hope for  the planet as Toller attempts to counsel  Mary and Michael he's forced to think  about his own beliefs and worry about  the current state of the human race  directed by Paul Schrader and starring  Ethan Hawke as Reverend Toller and  Amanda Seyfried as Mary first reformed  refuses to wrap up neatly for the viewer  after watching first reform you can't  help but wonder if the doomsday  believers have a point after all every  tiny insect the whole world is a  manifestation of his holy presence I  think this is an issue where the church  can lead annihilation is one of those  genre-bending films that combines the  best of science fiction and horror into  a single compelling storyline Cain  played by Oscar Isaac is the only  survivor of an expedition into the  shimmer an electromagnetic field that's  spreading on the southern coast of the  United States what really happened while  he was inside and was it really Kane who  came back a scientist played by Natalie  Portman is determined to find out with  her own expedition as she ventures into  the shimmer herself nothing is quite  what it seems in me  who will be in all of us the happy time  murders follows a human and puppet  detective team as they try to figure out  who's behind the recent murders of  retired sitcom stars with puppets  engaging in everything from violent  crime to petty theft and raunchy jokes  it's like a twisted look at your  childhood favorites you won't be able to  look at a puppet quite the same way  after watching the happy time murders  sure you've got to give the cast and  crew props for their willingness to get  creative in the medium but it's hard not  to wonder why this story had to be told  with puppets which is truly baffling in  Madeline's Madeleine Helena Howard stars  as Madeleine a young woman who joins a  prestigious physical theatre troupe her  intensely ambitious director pushes her  to explore her family trauma through her  art particularly her relationship with  her mother but Madeline's performance  begins to cross from art into life until  the two become completely  indistinguishable at its core Madeline's  Madeleine is a film about acting it  represents a look inside the mind of an  artist  complete with dizzying dramatic  cinematography the characters explore  identity through performance and the  movie encourages viewers to consider how  much of our own identities are authentic  parts of who we are and how much is  merely a performance for others excuse  me  yeah okay great in the suspenseful  thriller unsane Claire Foy plays Sawyer  a woman who moves away from home to  escape her stalker but even after she  thinks she's in the clear  Sawyer struggles to move on and meets  with a counselor to dig into the healing  process unfortunately she ends up being  tricked into a long-term stay in a  psychiatric institution where she makes  a terrifying discovery watching unsane  will have you questioning everyone  around you four days after your name is  Sawyer Valentini you know that already  by signing this you've